Q27: Constructors in Dart
What is required?
Create a class called Employee with four properties:
•	name
•	employeeId
•	department
•	salary
Use a parameterized constructor and a named constructor.
Dart Program

class Employee {

  // Properties
  String name;
  int employeeId;
  String department;
  double salary;

  // Parameterized constructor
  Employee(this.name, this.employeeId, this.department, this.salary);

  // Named constructor
  Employee.intern()
      : name = "Isaac",
        employeeId = 0,
        department = "Training",
        salary = 0.0;

  // Method
  void displayEmployee() {
    print("Name: $name");
    print("Employee ID: $employeeId");
    print("Department: $department");
    print("Salary: $salary");
    print("-------------------");
  }
}

void main() {

  // Creating employee objects
  Employee employee1 = Employee("isaac", 101, "IT", 500000);

  Employee employee2 = Employee("sharifu", 102, "Finance", 400000);

  // Using named constructor
  Employee employee3 = Employee.intern();

  // Display employee information
  employee1.displayEmployee();
  employee2.displayEmployee();
  employee3.displayEmployee();
}
Explanation:
1. Properties
String name;
int employeeId;
String department;
double salary;
These variables store employee information.
2. Parameterized constructor
Employee(this.name, this.employeeId, this.department, this.salary);
This constructor receives values when we create an object.
Example:
Employee employee1 = Employee("ORGAN", 101, "IT", 500000);
3. this keyword
this refers to the current object.
Example:
Employee(this.name);
It assigns the given name to the object's name property.
4. Named constructor
Employee.intern()
    : name = "Intern",
      employeeId = 0,
      department = "Training",
      salary = 0.0;
A named constructor has its own name and can give default values to an object.
5. Calling named constructor
Employee employee3 = Employee.intern();
This creates an intern employee using the default values.
summary to remember
Concept	Meaning
Constructor	-Initializes an object
Parameterized constructor	-Receives values
this	-Refers to the current object
Named constructor	-Constructor with a special name
displayEmployee()	-Displays employee information
Remember:
•	Employee() = Normal constructor.
•	Employee.intern() = Named constructor.
•	this = Refers to the current object's properties.
Q28: Encapsulation in a Banking System
What is required?
Create a banking program that protects account balance from unauthorized changes.
We will use:
•	Class
•	Private variable
•	Getter
•	Setter
•	Deposit method
•	Withdraw method
Dart Program

class BankAccount {

  // Private variable
  double _balance = 0;

  // Getter
  double get balance {
    return _balance;
  }

  // Setter
  set balance(double amount) {
    if (amount > 0) {
      _balance = amount;
    } else {
      print("Invalid balance!");
    }
  }

  // Deposit method
  void deposit(double amount) {
    if (amount > 0) {
      _balance += amount;
      print("Deposit successful!");
    } else {
      print("Invalid deposit amount!");
    }
  }

  // Withdraw method
  void withdraw(double amount) {
    if (amount > 0 && amount <= _balance) {
      _balance -= amount;
      print("Withdrawal successful!");
    } else {
      print("Insufficient balance or invalid amount!");
    }
  }
}

void main() {

  // Creating object
  BankAccount account = BankAccount();

  // Setting initial balance
  account.balance = 1000;

  // Deposit money
  account.deposit(500);

  // Withdraw money
  account.withdraw(200);

  // Display account balance
  print("Current Balance: ${account.balance}");
}
Output:
Deposit successful!
Withdrawal successful!
Current Balance: 1300.0
Explanation:
1. Private variable
double _balance = 0;
•	_balance is a private variable.
•	The underscore (_) makes it private within its Dart library.
•	It helps protect data from direct changes.
2. Getter
double get balance {
  return _balance;
}
A getter is used to read the value of a private variable.
Example:
print(account.balance);
3. Setter
set balance(double amount) {
  if (amount > 0) {
    _balance = amount;
  }
}
A setter is used to change a variable's value.
In this example, it only accepts positive values.
Example:
account.balance = 1000;
4. Deposit
void deposit(double amount) {
  if (amount > 0) {
    _balance += amount;
  }
}
It adds money to the account balance.
5. Withdraw
void withdraw(double amount) {
  if (amount > 0 && amount <= _balance) {
    _balance -= amount;
  }
}
It removes money from the account.
It does not allow the user to withdraw more money than the available balance.
6. Display balance
print(account.balance);
It uses the getter to read and display the account balance.
Summary – Remember
Concept	Meaning
Encapsulation	-Protecting data
Private variable	-Variable starting with _
Getter	-Reads private data
Setter	-Changes private data
Deposit	-Adds money
Withdraw	-Removes money
+=	-Adds a value
-=	-Subtracts a value

